{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Wolven in Yellowstone: jacht en voedselrelaties","claim":"The piece concerns Yellowstone wolves, their hunting, and food relationships.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"In Yellowstone, average pack size is 11.8 individuals.","narrative":"Yellowstonewolven zijn sociale roofdieren: ze jagen in groepen en hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title's subject and themes are supported by the source and echoed in the narrative.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven leven in sociale familiegroepen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves live in social family groups.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family, with older members (often the alpha male and alpha female) and subordinates, each having individual personality traits and roles within the pack.","narrative":"Introduce Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately summarizes the source's description of packs.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Ze jagen samen","claim":"Yellowstone wolves hunt together.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey, defense of territory and prey carcasses, and even survival benefits to infirmed individuals.","narrative":"met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source explicitly identifies group hunting of large prey.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"en zorgen voor hun jongen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves cooperate in caring for their young.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey, defense of territory and prey carcasses, and even survival benefits to infirmed individuals.","narrative":"en zorg voor jongen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately conveys the source's reference to cooperative care of offspring.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Een prooidier dat door wolven wordt gedood, biedt bovendien voedsel aan andere dieren: van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","claim":"Animals including ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates benefit by feeding on wolf kills.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills. For example, when wolves kill an elk, ravens and magpies arrive almost immediately. Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"Hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source names these animals as benefiting from or consuming wolf-kill remains.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Ook de samenstelling van de prooien veranderde.","claim":"The composition of wolves' prey changed over time.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"De veranderde samenstelling van hun prooi in verschillende perioden","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes a change in the composition of wolf prey across periods.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Onder de dieren die wolven in de vroege winter doodden, verschoof de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden tussen 1995–2000 en 2001–2007.","claim":"Among wolves' early-winter prey, the relative proportions of elk calves and bull elk shifted between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"Gebruik daarnaast de verschuiving in de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source gives the initial proportions and says the ratio reversed in the later period. The piece does not invent later percentages.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"De bron verbindt die verandering met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","claim":"The source links the change in prey composition to prey availability and vulnerability.","category":"attribution","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de bron verbindt die veranderingen met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately attributes the source's stated interpretation without asserting a single cause.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"Veranderingen in predatie en de effecten daarvan op elanden vallen samen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, het beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","claim":"Changes in wolf predation and their effects on elk are associated with other factors, including other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather (e.g. drought, winter severity).","narrative":"veranderingen in predatie en de effecten op elanden samenvallen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes these factors as linked to changes in predation and impacts on prey; “vallen samen met” does not overstate a single-factor cause.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."},{"excerpt":"De waarnemingen beschrijven wolven als onderdeel van sociale en voedselrelaties, niet als de enige bepalende kracht.","claim":"The observations portray wolves as part of social and food relationships, not as the sole force determining outcomes.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family","narrative":"Zo blijft het verhaal bij een complex samenspel, niet bij wolven als enige bepalende kracht.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This is a supported synthesis: the source describes social pack life, wolf kills feeding other animals, and predation and elk impacts as linked to multiple interacting factors.","materiality":"No material discrepancy."}]}

